<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yeshe-Ö</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 10th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Lha Lama Yeshe-Ö</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yeshe-Ö was a king of Guge in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> in the late 10th century who became a monk and devoted his reign to restoring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tholing-monastery">Tholing Monastery</a> in 996 and sent young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rinchen-zangpo">Rinchen Zangpo</a>, to study in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. Tradition holds that he died a captive of the Qarluqs, and his successors continued his policy by inviting the Indian teacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atisha">Atisha</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yeshe-o.jpg" alt="Yeshe-Ö" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artistUnknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yeshe-%C3%96.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
